~This is an in-process branch for experiments using instance instead of static methods on Operon classes.~
This branch is ready for review, and we did *NOT* switch to using instance methods

This PR adds `WorkflowContext.proxy` method that takes an Operon Operations class and returns an object that has directly invocable versions of all transaction and communicator methods. Instead of writing `wfCtxt.transaction(SomeClass.someMethod, p1, p2)`, the developer can write `proxy.someMethod(p1, p2)`. The generated proxy object automatically maps the developer's call to `wfCtxt.transaction` or `wfCtxt.external` as appropriate.

Additionally, TypeScript type mapping is used to provide a developer-time experience for using the generated proxy. The type of the proxy class only includes the transaction and communicator methods from the provided class type & the initial context parameter is removed from the generated method signature. So the workflow callable operations are available via intellisense on the proxy object and the method signatures can be verified at compile time.

From the examples/hello/userFunctions file:

```ts
export class Hello {

  @OperonWorkflow()
  @GetApi('/greeting/:name')
  static async helloWorkflow(wfCtxt: WorkflowContext, name: string) {
    const hello = wfCtxt.proxy(Hello);
    const encodedName = btoa(name);
    const decodedName = await hello.helloExternal(encodedName);
    return await hello.helloFunction(decodedName);
  }

  // no change to how communicator or transaction methods are written
  @OperonCommunicator()
  static async helloExternal(commCtxt: CommunicatorContext, encodedName: string) {
    try {
      const url = `https://httpbin.org/base64/${encodeURIComponent(encodedName)}`;
      const res = await fetch(url);
      return await res.text();
    } catch {
      commCtxt.warn("httpbin/base64 failed, decoding locally");
      return atob(encodedName);
    }
  }

  @OperonTransaction()
  static async helloFunction(txnCtxt: TransactionContext, name: string) {
    const greeting = `Hello, ${name}!`
    const { rows } = await txnCtxt.pgClient.query<{ greeting_id: number }>("INSERT INTO OperonHello(greeting) VALUES ($1) RETURNING greeting_id", [greeting])
    return `Greeting ${rows[0].greeting_id}: ${greeting}`;
  }
}
```

The *original* goal was to enable developers to write code something like this:

```ts
export class Hello extends OperonBase {
  @OperonTransaction()
  async helloFunction(name: string) {
    const greeting = `Hello, ${name}!`
    
    // NOTE: transaction context available via OperonBase protected property
    const { rows } = await this.txCtx.pgClient.query<{ greeting_id: number }>("INSERT INTO OperonHello(greeting) VALUES ($1) RETURNING greeting_id", [greeting])
    return `Greeting ${rows[0].greeting_id}: ${greeting}`;
  }

  @OperonWorkflow()
  async helloWorkflow(name: string) {
    // NOTE: custom this object wraps @OperonTransaction/Communicator method in calls to ctx.transaction/external 
    return this.helloFunction(name);
  }
}
```

